This is my bike:
[feat. coffee I couldn't be bothered to move]($image.asset('my-bike.jpeg').size(800,0))

It holds a few notable qualities:
* I've never seen the same model in Amsterdam, which is a pretty impressively rare feat.
I'm sure one exists, I've just never shared a rack with it.
* The rear brakes squeal like a motherfucker because I'm too lazy to clean the disk
properly (and it's been cold out so I don't want to do bike maintenance).
* The bell looks like a little blue bear because I pulled the cheapest one possible off
the shelf. It also does not work very well so usually my shouting is the bell.
* The derailleur is definitely a little out of alignment but I only change gears like once
a week so it doesn't really matter (perks of pancake land).

I love it. It's one of my favorite possessions. When other Americans ask me almost
jokingly if I bike everywhere I tell them yes without batting an eye because to think of
not biking everywhere is crazy. I can cross the entire city in under half an hour (under
20 minutes if Centraal Station isn't busy and/or it's really cold outside). I can go from
my building to pretty much any location in Amsterdam door to door without having to bother
with gas, or pay for parking, or worry about rush hour (ok to be fair there is still
obviously rush hour, but bikes don't really traffic jam in the same way cars do).

Myriad literature has been written about Dutch city design and making human centric
cities, and I'm not going to rehash any of the things that make it so effective here, but
I will tell you that I will never willingly go back to a car commute.

This is especially fun for me because I grew up riding bikes with my parents and
grandfather many many weekends out of the year (I rode in [El Tour de Tucson](https://eltourdetucson.org/) a few years).
I also commuted to and from work in Salt Lake City for about 5-6 months entirely by bike,
through a particularly brutally hot summer. Both Tucson and Salt Lake have vibrant cycling
communities, but even still I would characterize both as more sport/recreation oriented,
even if there are plenty of commuters too. It's hard to explain to people just how
ubiquitous bikes are here in the Netherlands; you don't really understand until you see it
in person that it's not just "a cultural thing." I mean it is, of course, but someone
choosing not to use a bike, even if they have a car, is just seen as impractical more than
anything.

I love riding my bike through the sun in the summer just for fun just as much as I love
struggling through the freezing wind and rain in the winter (in hindsight of course, in
the moment I am very unhappy). I cannot imagine living without it at this point. Of all
the things I love about living in a place that (generally) puts people first, my bike
might be the thing I love most.
